WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.137 --> 00:00:02.910
(crowd chanting in Chinese)

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:05.173
- [Narrator] People across
China are not happy.

00:00:05.173 --> 00:00:06.720
(crowd chanting in Chinese)

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:07.890
- [Narrator] And their anger exploded

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:10.098
on the streets for several days.

00:00:10.098 --> 00:00:12.720
(crowd chanting in Chinese)

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.909
From residents fed up with
lockdowns in far West China.

00:00:15.909 --> 00:00:17.370
(people speaking in Chinese)

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:18.203
- [Narrator] To students

00:00:18.203 --> 00:00:20.430
at prestigious universities in Beijing.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:22.920
Nearly three years into
the pandemic, protestors

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:25.650
from different walks of life
have been pushed to the brink,

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:28.110
a mid strict COVID controls
that have hurt livelihoods

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:30.047
and stifled the economy.

00:00:30.047 --> 00:00:31.560
(crowd shouting in Chinese)

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:32.393
- [Narrator] Here's how one

00:00:32.393 --> 00:00:34.080
of the largest nationwide demonstrations

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:36.210
in decades happened in China,

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:38.490
and how this fury creates
a political dilemma

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:40.173
for Xi Jinping's leadership.

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:44.040
The anger was sparked by a fire

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:46.519
in an apartment building
on Thursday in Urumqi,

00:00:46.519 --> 00:00:49.590
capital of the northwestern
region of Xinjiang

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:51.981
where officials said 10 people died.

00:00:51.981 --> 00:00:55.140
(crowd chanting in Chinese)

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:56.730
- [Narrator] The next day, residents upset

00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:59.820
by the deadly fire took their
frustrations to the streets,

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:01.620
having also struggled with lockdowns

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:03.030
for more than three months.

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:04.290
They said COVID restrictions

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:06.630
like barriers had delayed rescuers.

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:08.340
Officials said parked cars got

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:10.053
in the way of moving more quickly.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.010
On Saturday, in a show of solidarity,

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:15.780
people in Shanghai held a vigil

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:17.523
to mourn victims of the fire.

00:01:18.828 --> 00:01:21.450
(crowd chanting in Chinese)

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:23.700
- [Narrator] By the evening,
larger groups began gathering

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:26.820
on a road named after
Urumqi, which became a center

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:29.223
of protests that followed
in the next few days.

00:01:30.211 --> 00:01:31.860
(dramatic music)

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:33.480
As the demonstrations expanded,

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:35.753
a call for change became louder.

00:01:35.753 --> 00:01:36.750
(crowd chanting in Chinese)

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:38.490
- [Narrator] That China's
strict pandemic measures

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:39.778
be rolled back.

00:01:39.778 --> 00:01:43.528
(crowd chanting in Chinese)

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:46.890
- [Narrator] By Sunday, as protests spread

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:48.240
to other major cities,

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:50.238
some of the demands turned political.

00:01:50.238 --> 00:01:53.988
(crowd chanting in Chinese)

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:59.393
- [Narrator] And pointed
directly at President Xi Jinping.

00:01:59.393 --> 00:02:03.060
(crowd chanting in Chinese)

00:02:09.064 --> 00:02:10.920
- [Narrator] blank sheets of
paper also began popping up

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:13.800
at demonstrations to
symbolize protestors' defiance

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.003
against censorship.

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:19.770
While censors worked quickly
to remove any indication

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:22.470
of unrest on Chinese
social media platforms,

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:25.293
officials set out to
quash any new protests.

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:28.350
On Monday, the government deployed scores

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:32.310
of officers and police cars
lined the streets of Beijing.

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:33.510
Plastic barriers went up

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:35.220
along the road where the first protests

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:36.843
outside of Xinjiang began.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:40.350
Chinese state media also
published commentaries

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:42.720
defending Xi's zero COVID approach,

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:45.603
saying the policy is essential
for protecting lives.

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:50.370
Though the streets are quieter
and some pandemic measures

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:53.190
in Xinjiang and Beijing
have eased since Sunday,

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:54.900
these protests present a conundrum

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:58.020
for Xi who just secured a
norm-breaking third term

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:00.720
and runs a regime focused on stability.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:02.610
If he relaxes COVID restrictions,

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:05.040
that could lead to a
new wave of infections,

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:07.620
and a possible spike in
deaths among a large part

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:10.667
of the elderly population
that isn't vaccinated.

00:03:10.667 --> 00:03:11.940
(crowd chanting in Chinese)

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:13.290
But if he holds steadfast,

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:16.419
it could create deeper grievances
against his leadership.

00:03:16.419 --> 00:03:20.169
(crowd chanting in Chinese)

